# Python bytecode and caches
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
*.egg-info/

# Virtual environments
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Runtime state, not source.
# data/ruleforge.db is ~43MB of local history, fixtures and mapping overrides.
# It is created on first run and is machine-specific, so it must never be committed.
data/*.db
data/*.sqlite
data/*.sqlite3

# Editor / agent tooling state
.kilo/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Local environment overrides
.env
.env.*

# RuleForge history: pasted detection rules and EVENT SAMPLES, which contain
# hostnames, usernames, source IPs and destination IPs. The server never
# transmits them, but git add -A from a repo with a remote would publish them.
# This is the one file RuleForge writes.
#
# THE PERMISSION CLAIM WAS FALSE ON WINDOWS AND IS NOW WRITTEN DOWN. This
# comment used to say "filesystem permissions are 0600" as though that were
# simply true everywhere. It is not:
#
#   POSIX   0600, set explicitly by `history._write` via `os.chmod` (and by
#           `mkstemp` before it). The explicit call is there so the guarantee
#           does not rest on a stdlib implementation detail.
#   win32   `os.chmod` has ONE bit -- the read-only flag -- and does not model
#           POSIX permission bits. The file is exactly as readable as the
#           directory holding it. The real control there is the inherited ACL:
#           `data/` sits under the analyst's own profile, whose default ACL
#           grants that user, SYSTEM and Administrators, and not Everyone.
#
# That is a weaker and differently-shaped guarantee than 0600. It is still the
# reason this rule matters, and `git check-ignore -v data/history.json` is the
# only thing that confirms the rule still matches after any move.
#
# The path is `data/`, NOT `ruleforge/data/`. The package was flattened and an
# ignore rule naming a directory that no longer exists matches NOTHING -- with no
# error, no warning, while `git add -A` starts offering the analyst's pasted
# rules and their machine's hostnames for commit.
data/

